# Vision

The World Wide Web started as a collection of plain text files. Each HTML file
was one page, and a link in one page pointed to another page. A person could
write a page in a text editor, and anyone could read its source. HTML was then
about as simple as Markdown is now.

The web later became a platform for applications. A web page today is often the
output of a program. Its source is written for a browser to run, and it is
difficult for a person or a program to read. The web does that job well, and
HyperMarkDown does not replace HTML.

The web still lacks a semantic level. A semantic level is a form in which a
page states its own meaning in its own source: what the page is about, which
other pages it refers to, and how its results are computed. AI agents now read
and write documents together with people, and they need this level in order to
work with what the documents say.

The vision of HyperMarkDown is a web of HyperMarkDown files that provides this
level. Each file is a plain text file that contains one page. HyperMarkDown
treats that page as a **unit of knowledge** and gives it a structure:

- The text of the page explains an idea in Markdown.
- A header at the top of the file states facts about the page, such as its
  subject or its status.
- Links point to other pages by their addresses.
- Queries inside the page compute part of its content.

A person can read the file in any text editor. A program or an AI agent can
read the same file, follow its links, and run its queries.

A research note, a design decision, a calculation, and an instruction to an
agent can all be pages in the same knowledge base. Each page has an address,
and each page can link to the others.

Markdown provides the readable text. HyperMarkDown adds the header, the
addresses, and the links. [HQL, the Hyper Query Language](https://hql-lang.org/),
provides the queries and the computations.

Today, HyperMarkDown connects the pages of one knowledge base. Connecting
separate knowledge bases into a web is proposed and not yet implemented. The
last sections of this page describe that step.

## A card is a unit of knowledge

HyperMarkDown calls one page a **card**. A card is one text file, usually with
the `.hmd` extension, and it has its own address. The body of the card contains
the explanation. The header can state facts such as a title, an author, a
subject, or a status. Links connect the card to other cards. A link can also
point to one named section or one named block inside a card.

Consider a card about a design decision. It can explain the problem, link to
the requirements, embed a shared definition, and include a query that lists
the work still needed. Someone reading the card sees the decision in context.
A program can read its metadata, follow its references, and recompute the list.

The useful unit is the whole card: the claim, its context, and the means of
working with it. A collection of these cards forms a knowledge base whose
structure is available alongside its prose.

Ordinary `.md` and `.markdown` files can also be cards: HyperMarkDown can link
to and embed them while leaving their contents as plain Markdown. An `.hmd`
file opts into the additional syntax. This lets an existing collection grow
into a connected knowledge base a page at a time.

## Knowledge can be composed

A definition often belongs in several explanations. An embed lets those
explanations share the same source:

```markdown
The request uses [[tokens]]. The shared definition is:

![[glossary/token#^definition]]
```

The link names a card; the embed brings a named block into the page. The same
mechanism can include a section or a whole card. When the definition changes,
the pages that embed it show the change on their next render.

Names have defined resolution rules, so a tool can identify the target of a
reference and report an ambiguity. An embedded passage keeps the meaning of
the links it had in its source card. That makes composition useful for more
than publishing: a larger explanation can be assembled from smaller pieces
without losing how those pieces relate to the knowledge around them.

## A page can ask questions of its knowledge base

HQL reads cards as values with a title, body, path, and metadata. It also
provides access to their links and graph. A query can select cards, examine
their properties, and produce a result inside another card.

The repository's example notebook contains four book cards. Two have
`status: todo` in their metadata. A reading-list cell over that vault is:

````markdown
```hql#eval
cards
| filter(c => c.metadata.status == "todo")
| sort(by = c => c.title)
| map(c => c.title)
```
````

Run against that vault, it produces:

```text
- Emma
- The Hobbit
```

Change a book's status and run the cell again: the reading list changes. The
question lives on the page where its answer is useful, and the book cards
remain the source of the answer.

This is a small example of **introspection**: a knowledge base can examine its
own contents. The same approach can build a subject index, collect decisions
with a particular status, or count the documents in a collection. The project
uses it for its own documentation: HQL cells read the issue cards and trackers
to generate views of the open work.

The [example notebook](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown/tree/main/examples/notebook)
includes cells that share results with one another, a reading list, and a
progress table. HQL's [Queries inside documents](https://hql-lang.org/latest/book/24-documents/)
explains how to run them and save their outputs.

## Computation belongs with its explanation

An executable card can carry the question, the query, the result, and the
author's interpretation in one document. A reader can inspect how a result was
obtained, change the query, and run it again. Other cells in the document can
use that result in further calculations.

HQL can also work with structured data outside the vault. A report can combine
its written explanation with a computation over that data, then make the
result available as part of the card. The knowledge base becomes a place where
observations are interpreted and connected to what is already known.

Saved cell output is ordinary document content. It remains readable without
running the computation, and links in that output participate in the card's
graph. The source query remains available when output is saved alongside it.
A saved result records a run; refreshing it requires another run. Opening a
page and executing its cells are separate actions.

## Knowledge can participate in its own revision

The larger possibility is a knowledge base that represents how it works and
uses those representations to improve its work. Requirements can be cards.
Procedures can be cards. Observations about their results can be cards too.
They can be linked, queried, compared, and revised together.

Imagine a design review. A card states the requirements and holds a query
that gathers relevant evidence. Running the query produces observations. A
person or an agent reviews them, revises the requirements or the procedure,
and runs it again. Each step contributes knowledge that the next step can
inspect.

That is the sense in which the system can **reflect on itself**: its own
descriptions and results become material for further reasoning and computation.
The human reader can follow the process through the same documents, inspect
the evidence, and redirect the work.

HQL's design for agents extends this idea to instructions stored as cards and
runs whose inputs, tool calls, and results can be inspected as values. This
remains design work. The blog essay
[From Rust to English to Strange Loops](https://hql-lang.org/blog/ai-oriented-programming-language/)
develops the motivation, and
[Agents inside documents](https://hql-lang.org/latest/book/26-agents-inside-documents/)
explores what it could look like in a card.

## Independent knowledge bases can form a web

Today, HyperMarkDown resolves card references inside one namespace: an
organized collection of cards with a shared naming context and boundary. That
gives each knowledge base a context in which names have a predictable meaning.

The proposed next step is to connect independently maintained namespaces. A
reference such as `[[research:methods/sampling]]` would name a card in another
collection. Project configuration would say which collection `research`
means. Its author could keep maintaining it at its source while other
knowledge bases refer to it or compose with it.

At that scale, the same idea extends outward: people read pages, programs
query knowledge, and agents gather evidence from independently authored
collections. The knowledge remains addressable at its source, with explicit
connections between collections. The proposed namespace system is described
in [[namespaces#Beyond one namespace|Namespaces]]; it is not implemented by
the current language standard.

## The foundation and the direction

Cards, metadata, resolved links, embeds, and HQL cells provide the foundation
today. They already let a knowledge base describe itself and compute views of
its contents. Agent workflows and references between namespaces are the
direction being explored.

The aim is a readable, inspectable, executable body of knowledge: one in which
an explanation can lead to a computation, a computation can yield new evidence,
and that evidence can change what we understand and do next.

For the precise document rules, read the
[[/wiki/hmd-lang-spec|Language Specification]]. For how cards acquire
names and share a context, continue with [[namespaces|Namespaces]].
